**Read:** [Manufacturing — The Open University](../sources/Manufacturing-Open-University.lingua#r_5WVG0HEJ30KYA0GMVCHEZBY9X0), manufacturing economics and process comparisons; the existing mechanical stock and production-resource accounts. Revisit material and energy balances from the foundations.

Distinguish feed mass, gross shaped output, accepted product, internal return, external recovery, emissions and changing inventory. For consecutive one-pass mass yields 0.90, 0.80 and 0.95, the combined yield is 0.684, so 1 kg accepted output requires about 1.462 kg initial feed under that boundary. Recycled scrap changes the balance and its chemistry; it does not make every rejected part costless.

**Work:** Close a complete batch and continuous-operation account, including warm-up, idle heating, carrier solvents, cooling and consumable tooling. Compare energy per accepted functional unit, not just furnace efficiency. Identify where an upstream composition certificate, moisture result or powder distribution determines a downstream process setting. Keep quantity uncertainty and lot variability separate from losses that were actually measured.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
